%N A400753 Row sums of the Riordan array (1/(1-3*x), x/(1-3*x)^3).
%C A400753 a(n) is the number of objects of size n consisting of one initial word followed by a sequence of blocks, where each block is a marker of size 1 followed by three words. Words are possibly empty and are over a 3-letter alphabet; the size of a word is its length.
%C A400753  The Riordan array (1/(1-3*x), x/(1-3*x)^3) = (u(x)/x, u(x)*u'(x)), where u(x) = x/(1-3*x), is the flip, in the sense of Bang et al., of the Riordan array (1/(1-3*x)^2, x/(1-3*x)^3) = (u'(x), u(x)*u'(x)). See A400754.
%H A400753 Caroline Bang, Matias von Bell, Eric Culver, Jessica Dickson, Stoyan Dimitrov, Rachel Perrier, and Sheila Sundaram, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL26/Bell/vonbell6.html">On sums, derivatives, and flips of Riordan arrays</a>, J. Integer Seq., Vol. 26 (2023), Article 23.2.7.
%H A400753 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (10,-27,27).
%F A400753 a(n) = Sum_{k=0..n} binomial(n+2*k, 3*k)*3^(n-k).
%F A400753 G.f.: (1-3*x)^2/(1-10*x+27*x^2-27*x^3).
%F A400753 a(n) = 10*a(n-1) - 27*a(n-2) + 27*a(n-3) for n >= 3, with a(0)=1, a(1)=4, a(2)=22.
%F A400753 a(n) = A400754(n) - 3*A400754(n-1) for n >= 1.
%t A400753 LinearRecurrence[{10, -27, 27}, {1, 4, 22}, 23]
%Y A400753 Cf. A052544 and A092467 (analogous row sums with 3 replaced by 1 and 2), A109955, A400754.
%K A400753 nonn,easy,new
%O A400753 0,2
%A A400753 _Ifeyinwa Madu_, Oct 05 2026
